      "math_academy_id": "q-341383",
      "knowledge_point_id": "0de0627b-2568-5c02-8928-d14344df5a2f",
      "knowledge_point": "Finding the Derivative of an Expression With Sine or Cosine Trigonometric Functions",
      "problem": "Find the derivative of $y=\\frac{5sin⁡x}{4}+\\frac{{x}^{4}}{4}.$\n\n$\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=$\n{{field-1}}",
      "worked_solution": "We know that $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(sin⁡x)=cos⁡x.$ Applying this formula, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{5sin⁡x}{4}+\\frac{{x}^{4}}{4}) \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{4}(cos⁡x)+\\frac{1}{4}(4{x}^{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{5cos⁡x}{4}+{x}^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "answer_fields": [
        {
          "key": "field-1",
          "type": "blank",
          "choices": [
