    {
      "math_academy_id": "q-47985",
      "knowledge_point_id": "0de0627b-2568-5c02-8928-d14344df5a2f",
      "knowledge_point": "Finding the Derivative of an Expression With Sine or Cosine Trigonometric Functions",
      "problem": "Find the derivative of $y=-2cos⁡x+{x}^{-2}.$",
      "worked_solution": "We know that $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(cos⁡x)=-sin⁡x.$ Applying this formula, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(-2cos⁡x+{x}^{-2}) \\\\  & =-2(-sin⁡x)+(-2){x}^{-2-1} \\\\  & =2sin⁡x-2{x}^{-3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
