      "math_academy_id": "e-3632",
      "knowledge_point_id": "711f198b-26b4-5185-812a-102329d49313",
      "knowledge_point": "Finding the Derivative of an Expression With the Tangent Trigonometric Function",
      "problem": "Find the slope of $y = 4\\tan x + 5x$ at $x = 0.$",
      "worked_solution": "The slope at $x=0$ is given by the value of the derivative $y'$ at $x=0.$ \n\nFirst, we compute the derivative, using the fact that $\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}}{\\textrm{d}x} (\\tan x) = \\sec^2 x.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(4tan⁡x+5x) \\\\  & =4{sec}^{2}⁡x+5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluating at $x=0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}(0) & =4{sec}^{2}⁡0+5 \\\\  & =4\\cdot 1+5 \\\\  & =9.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope is $9.$",
